{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1526661-how-strong-is-the-god-of-swords","id":1526661,"title":"How Strong is the God of Swords?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-12-11T01:35:01-08:00","id":1526661,"title":"How Strong is the God of Swords?","content":"Just on a whim, I'd been looking up the statblock for Five Metal Tang, the god of Swords in the Sidereal manuscript. Despite a relatively low number of charms, they had some crazy strong effects which led me to wonder just how strong they were. To get an idea of it, I rolled up a couple of white-room battles against the biggest measuring stick the system has (short of a third circle demon), Ahlat.<br /> <br /> For the purposes of this battle, I won't be using any stunts for either combatant, and I also won't use FMTs Lord of Blades charm or Ahlat's Whispering Warrior Souls for anything other than the =5 initiative break boost, since those powers are meant to be a bit subjective and freeform in what options they can give. As such, both might be a bit weaker than they'd otherwise be.<br /> <br /> Battle:<br /> Roll JB - Ahlat spends 10m boosting his, FMT has nothing to boost theirs.<br /> Ahlat rolls 21 dice, gets 11 successes. FMT rolls 14 and gets 6<br /> <br /> Round 1:<br /> Initiative: <br /> Ahlat 14<br /> FMT 9<br /> <br /> Motes:<br /> Ahlat: 110<br /> FMT: 120<br /> <br /> Just not enough for Ruin Smiting Strike, so Ahlat goes for a withering attack, using Boundless War God Arete to boost it, and Horned God's Prowess to try and do more damage. He spends a Willpower to boost it to, but FMT just responds by spending a Willpower to boost their Parry, canceling each other out. FMT also uses God Metal Scabbard Stance to try and reduce the incoming damage.<br /> 24 dice end up being 16 successes, very lucky. That is 8 successes over the Parry of 8, but HGP doubles the threshold successes, giving Ahlat 16 threshold successes, ouch. HGP will also double 7s on the damage roll, though GMSS will cause 1s to cancel successes. That's 22 damage post soak.<br /> 20 Successes on the withering damage dice and not a single one rolled. Things are not looking good for FMT. Ahlat crashes FMT, going up to 40 Initiative.<br /> <br /> Still, FMT has the chance to turn things around, and begins by using Principle of Motion to flurry two attacks. They're both going to be Withering, and both going to use Steel Sheering Finesse to add successes and ignore Soak. That kinda messes up Ahlat's biggest defensive charm, Aegis of Invincible Authority. He'll still use it for the post Soak bonus however.<br /> First attack with 14 dice and 3 auto succeses gets 11 successes, which beats Ahlat's Parry by 5. 25 damage from that, but Ahlat halves it.<br /> 6 withering damage, not even enough to get FMT out of crash. Still, it's got Ahlat a little worried, so this time, instead of using Aegis, he'll use Whirlwind Dervish Defense, spending 5 initiative to boost his defense up. But he's also got an onslaught penalty to deal with.<br /> Second attack only gets 8 successes, not enough to beat Ahlat's boosted defense, he's safe.<br /> <br /> Round 2:<br /> Initiative:<br /> Ahlat 29<br /> FMT -4<br /> <br /> Motes:<br /> Ahlat: 91<br /> FMT: 91<br /> <br /> Alhat begins and he's going all in with BWGA, Ruin-Smiting Strike, and Blood Kisses Sand. 24 dice again vs FMTs Parry, but with double 9s too. Again, both will spend Willpower to boost the attack roll and parry respectively.<br /> Only 13 successes. Looks like he used up all his luck last round. That's still enough to do 33 dice of damage with double 9s however.<br /> 20 successes on the decisive roll, that's really hurting FMT, they're well into the -2s now. Not a ton of 9s or 10s on the damage roll, which means Ahlat is resetting to base with only a +5.<br /> <br /> FMT gets to go, and will Flurry again, using SSF once more as well. Ahlat would really rather not spend Initiative here, so he uses Aegis again.<br /> Only 12 dice this time due to wound penalty, but the auto successes bring it up to 9 successes, beating Ahlat's parry.<br /> 11 dice do 7 withering damage, putting Ahlat very close to being crashed.<br /> FMT will use SSF again, really wanting to crash Ahlat. Ahlat is just going to use Aegis again to try and mitigate the damage<br /> 9 successes on the second attack, and the onslaught means this gets a tiny bit more damage this time.<br /> 2 withering successes on 12 dice. It's technically enough to crash him, but wow. No Initiative Shift here because FMT was already out of crash from the first attack this turn.<br /> (Ordinarily, FMT would suffer the bleeding and initiative loss effect from BKS here, but they have a special merit, Born of the Blade, which negates crippling effects or effects that targets organs nerves or the like. I'm going to say that it negates that effect)<br /> <br /> Round 3:<br /> Initiative: <br /> Ahlat: -1<br /> FMT: 12<br /> <br /> Motes:<br /> Ahlat: 41<br /> FMT: 72<br /> <br /> FMT goes first this time using PoM to Flurry again, but this time leading a Decisive attack with Peerless Sword Flourish to take advantage of Ahlat being crashed. Ahlat won't let them though! Using Auroch King's Wrath, Ahlat reflexively rolls Join Battle with double 9s.<br /> He rolls great, gets 9 successes, is no longer crashed. Ahlat doesn't use any further defensive techniques, content to tank the attack from here.<br /> FMT gets... 3 successes on 12 dice. They actually miss, even with the -2 onslaught Ahlat is under.<br /> Undeterred, FMT will use their second flurry attack for a withering attack with SSF again. This time, Ahlat is going to use WWD for 5i and Aegis to protect himself. He really doesn't want to get crashed here.<br /> FMT finally gets a good roll, 13 successes, well over the 8 parry Ahlat could raise.<br /> The Aegis and WWD drop the damage down massively, however, lowering it to 2 damage.<br /> <br /> Ahlat will make a withering attack, using BWGA and HGP again, but he can only give 5 motes for BWGA this time due to his low initiative. FMT will use GMSS to try and mitigate damage and Peerless Guardian Excellence to negate penalties.<br /> Ahlat gets 9 successes on 19 dice. Not terrible, but even with HGP, it's not a ton this time, only counting as 4 threshold successes and not doubling any damage dice beyond the usual 10s.<br /> 10 dice gets 6 damage, and no ones to subtract successes.<br /> <br /> Round 4:<br /> Initiative: <br /> Ahlat: 12<br /> FMT: 6<br /> <br /> Motes:<br /> Ahlat: 27<br /> FMT: 41<br /> <br /> Ahlat decides to end it, both of them are getting tired by this point, but FMT's metal body might give them the endurance to push through. Ahlat uses Whispering Warrior Souls to get an immediate initiative break of +5, letting him use Ruin Smiting Strike again. He also uses BGA for 10m as well. FMT simply uses GMSS again. Both spend a Willpower to boost attack and Parry again.<br /> Unfortunately, Ahlat rolls kinda bad this time, 10 successes vs 6 Parry, and only 2 9s on the roll to add damage. Ruin Smitting might have backfired here.<br /> And it did <b>indeed</b>. 4 success on 14 dice, but 5 1s turn that to no successes. No damage today. He still rolled 1 10, so Ahlat gets +1 when resetting to base, but it's not looking good.<br /> <br /> FMT will PoM to Flurry, starting with SSF.<br /> 9 successes on the attack will beat Ahlat's Parry.<br /> 10 withering damage this time, crashing Ahlat once again.<br /> The second attack is going to be a decisive. No PSF this time, because FMT is completely out of willpower.<br /> 7 Successes leaves that a clean hit vs Ahlat's parry.<br /> 22 decisive damage dice, but with nothing to boost it, it's only 9 damage. Ahlat is still in the -8s<br /> <br /> Round 5:<br /> Initiative:<br /> Ahalt: -3<br /> FMT: 3<br /> <br /> Motes:<br /> Ahlat: 7<br /> FMT: 20<br /> <br /> FMT gets to go again, but here we see the real weakness - they're best attack vs Ahlat, Steel Sheering Finesse, needs Willpower, and FMT doesn't have any left. Without any other way to boost their attacks, FMT will just make a basic withering attack.<br /> They still hit, 6 successes is more than enough with the onslaught Ahlat has. But it's reduced just down to 5 overwhelming<br /> 2 damage is a start.<br /> <br /> Ahlat takes his turn, just making a withering attack, trying to conserve his motes and willpower at this point. FMT will use PGE to ignore penalties and block on a tie.<br /> Which matters because Ahlat only got 7 successes. The attack is blocked.<br /> <br /> Round 6:<br /> Initiative: <br /> Ahlat: -5<br /> FMT: 6<br /> <br /> Motes:<br /> Ahlat: 12<br /> FMT: 19<br /> <br /> FMT makes another withering attack<br /> 5 successes, not enough to beat Ahlat's Parry this time.<br /> <br /> Ahlat makes his strike, FMT uses PGE again.<br /> This time Ahlat gets 10 successes, giving 3 threshold damage<br /> ... I have no words, Ahlat gets 9 successes on 9 withering dice of damage. Clearly, heaven favored him this battle<br /> Initiative Shift! JB roll goes poorly, but that's still 2 more initiative on top of everything else he got.<br /> Decisive Attack, Ahlat uses BKS, 2m on GWA, and a Willpower. FMT uses GMSS to try and survive.<br /> Ahlat just hits, 6 successes on 16 dice and an auto success working with the wound penalty and onslaught.<br /> No damage however, way too many 1s rolled to counteract the 1 successful dice of 14.<br /> <br /> Round 7:<br /> Initiative:<br /> Ahlat: 3<br /> FMT: -3<br /> <br /> Motes:<br /> Ahlat: 5<br /> FMT: 16<br /> <br /> Ahlat makes another withering attack, using HGP since FMT can't get rid of the defense penalties while crashed. He also spends a Willpower. FMT uses GMSS to try and mitigate damage.<br /> 11 successes, 8 threshold vs the 3 parry, which gets doubled to 16 thanks to HGP, and then gives db 7s on the damage roll.<br /> 21 withering damage from 24 dice, and that's after 3 1s subtracted. I think this is the end.<br /> FMT makes a withering attack.<br /> 6 successes hits, but how much can it do when reduced to overwhelming?<br /> 2 withering damage, not much<br /> <br /> Round 8:<br /> Initiative:<br /> Ahlat: 23<br /> FMT: -24<br /> <br /> Motes:<br /> Ahlat: 6<br /> FMT: 13<br /> <br /> Ahlat makes a decisive attack, spending 6m on GWA. FMT uses GMSS to se if they can survive.<br /> 12 successes means it hits.<br /> ... and then 3 damage. From 23 dice (admittedly, GMSS took away another 3, but still!) Dear Incarna, FMT <i>still</i> isn't in their -4s yet!<br /> <br /> FMT makes a withering attack<br /> 7 successes, it hits.<br /> 3 withering damage... which actually crashes Ahlat. Initiative Shift! That was pretty much the only hope they had.<br /> 6 successes on JB.<br /> No reason not to make a decisive here, so they'll do so<br /> 8 successes on the decisive, it hits.<br /> only 3 decisive damage from the 9 dice, but at least Ahlat is into his -1s now<br /> <br /> Round 9:<br /> Initiative:<br /> Ahlat: 0<br /> FMT: 3<br /> <br /> Motes:<br /> Ahlat: 5<br /> FMT: 10<br /> <br /> FMT makes another withering attack<br /> 2 successes on 12 dice will miss, even with -2 onslaught and -1 wound penalty.<br /> <br /> Ahlat makes his own withering attack, using HGP. This time, FMT isn't crashed and uses PGE.<br /> But it doesn't matter because he got 10 successes. With the threshold being doubled, thats 6 threshold and db 9s on damage<br /> 8 withering damage successes, another Initiative Shift!<br /> 7 successes on JB. His Initiative shoots up and he makes a reflexive decisive. He'll spend his last mote and last willpower. FMT no longer has enough motes to defend with.<br /> 3 successes, somehow he missed.<br /> <br /> Round 10:<br /> Initiative:<br /> Ahlat: 18<br /> FMT: -5<br /> <br /> Motes:<br /> Ahlat: 5<br /> FMT: 11<br /> <br /> Ahlat makes another decisive attack, spending 5m on GWA.<br /> 9 success, he hits<br /> 5 successes on the decisive damage roll. Technically, FMT isn't incapacitated yet, but with his last -4 filled in, I think he'll concede the fight there. Ahlat still has 18 health levels left before his own incapacitated level would be filled.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> So, that battle took longer than I expected. Some of the rolls were kinda wonky there. Both of them spent pretty much all of their motes, and all of their willpower (even from their cults!). I made a few mistakes on each side (FMT should have conserved Willpower, and Ahlat shouldn't have gone for the second Ruin Smite), so despite how much Ahlat seemed to dominate, Five Metal Tang actually pushed him pretty hard. I think they could have done a lot better if Ahlat hadn't gotten so lucky at the beginning but I believe overall it was a pretty good showing of what each of these powerful gods can do.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude0e","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26722-speeddemon","name":"speeddemon","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-17T13:34:25-08:00","id":1526997,"title":"","content":"Cool write up! I love that they were both utterly spent on Motes by the end.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6230-katana1515","name":"Katana1515","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6230_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"280"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}